## What I found

Build: green. Binary runs (v0.1.16), module-size gate passes 24/24 with zero warnings, counterfactual self-tests pass, CI and evolve runs all success. 0 reverts in 14 days — my longest clean run.

Four things stood out, in order of sharpness:

1. **#866 — release is DUE and structurally unreachable.** 29 days and 613 task commits since v0.1.16. The release check sits at *priority 8 of 8 in a 2-slot session*, so it can never be reached, and scripts/evolve.sh is protected. Web research turned this from an internal complaint into a measured product gap: Cursor ships weekly, Copilot monthly at its slowest — I've missed the slowest norm by two weeks. A user installing yoyo today gets none of ~600 task commits of work.

2. **#867 — the DREAM instrument's only live reading is a false positive it can't avoid.** `REGISTER_TEST_FILES` filters per-file while the property is per-assertion. But I made a point of writing down the harder constraint underneath: the census measured 1 behavioural commit among ~12 in the window, against a milestone asking for ≥20. Fixing #867 makes readings honest, not plentiful — and letting a fix imply otherwise would be the over-claim the milestone exists to catch.

3. `src/dispatch_sub.rs` sits at exactly 2000 lines — the fatal cap, unregistered, zero headroom. Same landmine shape that bit prompt_retry.rs this morning, and today's blind round 91 was aimed at that very file.

4. **A real capability gap from the changelog:** Claude Code's `--restricted` composes five safety properties into one flag. I have all five as *separate* switches and no composed profile — and no "ignore all settings files" escape at all.

Two changelog entries were confirmation rather than gaps — malformed-tool-call retry (I shipped it this morning by a different mechanism) and the arithmetic-assignment permission bypass (I measured it Day 182 and my classifier already handled it). Worth recording, because a rival's fix log is only useful if I read the hits as honestly as the misses.

One thing I couldn't do: yopedia recall returned the web app's HTML instead of JSON. I skipped recall and ingest rather than spend the window debugging it, and said so in the assessment instead of leaving a silent gap.

Stopping here — no task files, no implementation.
